-- ============================================================================
-- Shared: same trick Main's own Instant Prompt feature uses -- a ProximityPrompt's
-- HoldDuration means fireproximityprompt has to actually sit through that whole hold
-- (some executor implementations do simulate the real hold, not an instant tap), and
-- RequiresLineOfSight can reject the trigger entirely depending on camera angle. Zeroing
-- both right before firing makes every prompt below trigger instantly and reliably instead
-- of however long/finicky its real configured hold actually is.
-- ============================================================================
local function MakePromptInstant(prompt)
	pcall(function()
		prompt.HoldDuration = 0
		prompt.RequiresLineOfSight = false
	end)
end

-- ============================================================================
-- Auto Task: TaskRemote2 pushes the world Part the server currently wants you at (same
-- object TaskLocal's own dumped script listens for) -- walk to it and trigger its prompt
-- the same way. Once triggered, the server pushes a task TYPE (1/2/3) over TaskRemote,
-- and TaskHandler's own already-running script builds the real minigame GUI in response --
-- this doesn't reimplement that minigame, it just plays it: reads the same text a person
-- would read and clicks the same buttons a person would click, so it's driving the actual
-- completion path (TaskHandler's own script is what fires the real TaskRemote:FireServer
-- true/false) rather than trying to fake a result and hope the server doesn't check.
--
-- Monitor/CodePad/Pressing all live as permanent template children directly under
-- StarterGui.TasksGui (confirmed by hand, not guessed) -- CodePad and Pressing get reused
-- in place (TaskHandler just flips CodePad.Visible / adds a "ButtonActive" clone under
-- Pressing), but Monitor gets :Clone()'d into a SECOND sibling also named "Monitor" that's
-- the one that actually becomes visible/interactive -- FindFirstChild("Monitor") isn't
-- reliable there since it can just as easily return the original, permanently-invisible
-- template instead of the live clone. That mismatch (waiting on the wrong Monitor, or
-- looking for Pressing nested under a "TaskHandler" folder that was never actually right --
-- Pressing is TasksGui's own direct child) is almost certainly what "teleported to the
-- next task and everything just disappeared" was: the detection below timed out doing
-- nothing, so the Heartbeat loop immediately re-triggered the same still-active prompt a
-- moment later and stomped whatever had just started opening.
-- ============================================================================
local TaskRow, TaskToggle = CreateFeatureRow(1, "Auto Task")

